e got is a  lot of people are saying voting machines  aren't hooked to the internet  can hack still happen how another very  good question so when you went to vote  as I'm sure many of you did you know  what you would see is you're not you're  not voting on a machine is connected to  anything these are standalone kind of  pop-up kiosks you know and if they're a  computer it's just a memory card you  know the memory card you pull out the  memory card and that's that's how the  votes are tallied if it's electronic you  know vote machine but there are still  ways that these things can be hacked I  think there's been in general a public  misperception about what's likely as to  be hacked on Election Day voting  machines are kind of eminent Lee  hackable these things are old they're  like very basic processors they're just  very hackable they're just not very well  designed machine so if you can get a  connection to one of these things and  researchers have demonstrated this over  and over you know you can hack these  things really really easily but the way  that these things would happen are more  at the tabulator side which are  computers that are connected to some  types of internets internet connections  so that they can transmit and calculate  the results so that's where if a tax  were to happen they would happen in that  area or in the transmission stage of  states reporting counties reporting  numbers to the states the you know the  next question is you know can hack still  happen and how well this is a you know  this gets to this idea of you know now  the election has happened we have our  president-elect some people are really  happy about it some people or not you  know art should we expect to see any  additional cyber fallout from this and  the answer is maybe if we talked about  before you know if there is any gap in  the reconciliation of paper ballots to  electronic tabulation that's going to be  a monster problem we're not expecting we  don't have any evidence that that's the  case but it's a possibility at this  point it's worth raising and it's worth  kind of keeping in the back your mind as  you watch some of these finalized  results come in but also there's a  really interesting thing that happened  six hours after Trump was named the  president-elect I'm going to bring up  another screen here that I think you all  might find interesting so this is a  report that came out today from a cyber  security firm called vleck City  now what vleck city is found which I  find to be fascinated remember we just  talked about how the denial of service  attacks on election I basically stopped  you know what we see here from vleck  city is six hours after Trump was named  president elect the same group that hit  the Democratic Party the same group that  hit you know Hillary Clinton's campaign  managers email this Russian group that  cyber security researchers know very  well they reactivated they basically  went silent after they were outed for  hacking the Democratic Party six hours  after Trump is named president-elect  they're back at it with this massive you  know spearfishing campaign against  American not you know non-government  organizations think tanks political  entities in Washington you know that  have a lot of people from the government  on staff that that talk to the  government a lot that have a lot of  influence in Washington and what this  firm other cyber security firm found was  these guys reactivated and they went on  this basically this hacking kind of  bombing spree you know attacking these  organizations with emails such as this  the shocking truth about election  rigging in the United States some of  these emails some of these emails  purported to come from a allegedly  hacked Harvard University email server  they purported to come from the Clinton  Foundation kind of offering an alleged  post-mortem on the attack very well  designed to tax and when the cyber  security researchers looked into it they  found it was basically the same attack  infrastructure that hit the DNC so you  know there were a number of examples of  this this one is again the shocking  truth about election rigging in the US  there were other examples as well this  one was you know the election outcome  could be revised obviously for people  who were unhappy with the results this  would be an email many people would  click on even though it looks kind of  crazy like efax solution like I'm not  familiar with is why would I click on it  nevertheless if it comes from a trusted  source or a source that you believe to  be trustworthy and it contains you know  an email subject line that you find  compelling you know this was again a  spear phishing campaign a targeted  directed sophisticated attack against  you know organizations that have  political influence or political  employees what's the takeaway from that  it's hard to know but you know the  timing couldn't be an  you know basically what it appears to be  is that whoever was doing this hacking  against the US political political  system kind of stood down for a while  and once the president-elect was named  they kind of reengaged and you know take  from that what you take from that what  you will however what this indicates is  this group is back at it in a serious  way we have another question coming in  can ballot scanners be hacked to read a  selection as another interesting  question because we saw some reports of  this on election night where people  would punch a button for let's say  Hillary and the machine would register  that is Trump a lot of people are  concerned about that as we all should be  however what we heard was that there's  actually a pretty reasonable explanation  for why that happens and you know if you  if you watch the show with any  regularity you'll hear me sound like a  broken record on this voting technology  is really really old you know in  technology terms when we're talking  about you know annual or kind of  biannual refresh cycles election  technology has been there you know since  Bush v gore and you know in 2000-2001  that time frame in the tech world that's  really really old and so what happens is  you know it's it's easier for the  technology to go bad than it is to hack  it so when it comes to optical scanners  and things like touch screens what winds  up happening a lot of times or sometimes  is the the analog sensors can can shift  these are is a piece of hardware they  can shift so when you're pressing one  button it's actually registering as  another so you know you want to you want  to approach some of these reports of  abnormalities with some grain of  skepticism because oftentimes what  happens is the technology gets old and  fails in a way that Miss registers some  of the results you know ballot scanners  in response to this so this viewers  question you know could they be hacked  to read one selection as another sure  these are all software applications that  run on these things so if you can get  access to one of these things you could  manipulate it in a certain way however  the broken record in me wants to say if  you're going to hack an election there  are two places you do it it's tabulator  or it's the reporting mechanisms for  counties to report results to states  that's where you can do things in bulk  there's another question here coming in  you know voting machines seem very old  are there any plans to improve them  subject near and dear to my heart we  just spent several months reporting a  story that ran on business week's cover  you know a month or so ago about the  state of voting technology and why  things go wrong we reported extensively  on a case in Memphis Tennessee where the  voting machine technology was so old  that it was just dropping votes and in  that case it was primarily from black  churches you know people would go vote  by the hundreds and their votes were  just dropping nobody would know that's  the danger of old technology you know in  terms of your the question are there  plans to improve them there's a lot of  desire to improve them these count no  county wants to have ancient technology  that's hard to maintain you know that  has errors that they know about hard to  fix the problem becomes the reason we  have the electronic voting system that  we do is after the bush v gore you know  court case the federal government  basically opened up four billion dollars  in funding for states and counties to  buy new systems we haven't seen another  one of those you know maybe after this  election the government will will  reconsider that but it's kind of  doubtful basically you're not going to  see a refresh of this this electronic  voting technology and states or counties  without the federal government stepping  in and saying here are several more  billion dollars to you know to refresh  your systems the there are some  interesting there are some interesting  approaches being tried Los Angeles  County is one of them where they're  trying to build their own system from  scratch they're saying we want to open  source the technology one we only use  the best of what Silicon Valley has  pioneered in terms of software  development hardware development that  you know buying hardware from China and  then putting your own applications on  top of it and you know they're using  that federal money they're saying we're  not going to buy any of these old  systems we're going to build our own and  use the federal money to do that it's a  very nascent experiment but it's a very  exciting one because if Los Angeles  could build a functioning system based  on open source technology that could be  iterated on and improved by members of  the public and members of the  cybersecurity community you know and  could be replicated you know throughout  the country so we wouldn't be beholden  to this old hackable vulnerable system  and you know that's really kind of the  hope of a lot of folks  look at this space is that you know if  you can open source if you can move to  more a more modern model of Technology  you know you look at this stuff and you  wouldn't even recognize it if you've  covered Silicon Valley or you follow you  know follow the technological revolution  we've had the semiconductor revolution  and the Google revolution things like  that you wouldn't even recognize the  stuff that you're voting on I mean it is  so kind of archaic in many ways that  there are efforts to push us forward but  those are still in the nascent phase so  you know with that I want to wrap this  up here you know by really by really  saying you know while we didn't see kind  of the Hat the big hack the hack that  wasn't on election night there are a lot  of interesting kind of sub stories to  follow here you know as we move forward  with you know with with Trump's plans to  take the White House and you know and as  these ballots are finalized my big  messages story's not over yet you know  there are still things that you know  that need to be resolved and reconciled  and it will be very interesting for  members of the public to watch this  group that hacked the DNC where they go  next and what they do with the  information that they hack will be very  revealing in terms of how this election  was tampered with allegedly by foreign  power and what that means for us as a  country what we do about that going  forward we've kind of seen an  unprecedented event here and not just  because of the election but what  happened before the election and how  hackers use is a part of it so with that  I want to thank everybody for for  viewing and we're going to be doing this  every week one o'clock eastern so please  you know join us join us next time and  if you miss any of the show and you want  to want to check it out it will be  archived you know on our Facebook and  YouTube pages so thanks for watching a  digital offense and we'll see you next  week
 

